WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.291 --> 00:00:02.290
STEPHANIE WONG: So we
have our Hipster Shop app.

00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:04.720
And we've been talking
a lot about Istio.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.360
But when you have Istio managing
microservices, why would

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.207
we need API
management and Apigee?

00:00:09.207 --> 00:00:11.290
MARUTI C.: Sure, I get
this question all the time.

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:14.367
In simple words, Istio is
great to manage microservices,

00:00:14.367 --> 00:00:16.450
but when developers decide
to share those services

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:18.620
to other teams of
external developers,

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:19.870
they need to publish an API.

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:22.310
A successful API needs
to be easily consumed.

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:24.054
That's really peer
management concept.

00:00:24.054 --> 00:00:26.470
With Apigee, you can easily
employ standard API management

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:27.340
capabilities.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:28.840
STEPHANIE WONG: Sounds like
a helpful set of features.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:30.430
Can you show me how
I can get this setup?

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:31.096
MARUTI C.: Sure.

00:00:31.096 --> 00:00:32.722
Let me show you how it works.

00:00:32.722 --> 00:00:35.152
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.300
Let's use Hipster
Shop app for the demo.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:44.420
Hipster Shop app is an internal
microservices application

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:47.380
and the best app where users
can browse items, add them

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.240
to the cart, and
purchase them, which

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:50.770
is running entirely
on Kubernetes

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:51.960
and managed by Istio.

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.840
This is the service architecture
of the Hipster Shop app.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.940
So far, the shipping
service is utilized only

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.810
by the internal microservices.

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:04.230
Now we want to expose the
shipping service as a tracking

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:05.940
API to the third
party developers,

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.200
so they can take
advantage of a service.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.650
Let's go back and expose
the shipping services

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.680
tracking API using
this virtual service.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.480
Tracking in the
API part is pointed

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:18.910
to their shipping service,
which is running on Kubernetes.

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:20.880
Once the service
is created, let's

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.700
access the API to see if
it is created successfully.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.600
And we can retrieve the tracking
details of the tracking ID.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.540
Once the necessary Apigee
provisioning is taken care of,

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:33.240
these definitions here are
to configure the Apigee Mixer

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.460
and Istio so that we can
start using the capabilities.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:38.790
Let's look at some of the
things we can do in Apigee.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:40.740
This configuration
tells Istio to send

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.450
all the required
attributes to Mixer

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.400
for each transaction,
which will be forwarded

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.152
to Apigee for
analytics reporting

00:01:46.152 --> 00:01:48.360
and apply rules to traffic
flowing through this Istio

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.390
service mesh.

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:53.370
Once the configurations
are set, let's

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:55.440
create a rule to
capture analytics

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.530
for all the inbound calls
to the microservices.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:01.110
This records all the
inbound defined fields

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:05.660
and forwards to Apigee
for analytics reporting.

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:07.160
Let's go to the
Apigee management UI

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:08.930
to access the outer
box reports to view

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:11.780
the metrics for all
the microservices

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:14.390
for an individual service.

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:16.850
This particular dashboard,
Proxy Performance,

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:20.750
displays the total traffic
success and failure metrics.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:23.210
There are out-of-box
reports and custom reports

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:25.100
to get various metrics
like error code

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:28.015
and latency and more.

00:02:28.015 --> 00:02:29.390
We have an API,
but we don't have

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:31.280
a way the developers
can start using

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:32.510
it to create developer apps.

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:34.490
To permit that,
let's create an API

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:36.440
from the Apigee
Edge Management UI.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:39.260
For this product, we'll
call it as Take5 product.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:40.790
This is a place
where the API team

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:44.010
can define the access level
settings, quarter settings,

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:46.520
including all of the
shipping service resources

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:48.387
that the developer
applications can access.

00:02:48.387 --> 00:02:49.970
This is a service
name of the shipping

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:51.480
service and Kubernetes.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.020
Once the API product is
created and available,

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:56.690
developers can go to the
Hipster Shop developer portal

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:59.171
to test the API and
create their apps.

00:02:59.171 --> 00:03:00.920
Let's look at the API
product and tracking

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:03.990
API that is published.

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:07.050
As there is no security,
any one that is logged in

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:10.110
can access the API and
return the tracking details.

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:12.240
In this case, the API
team is unaware of who

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.094
is accessing the API and is
unable to enforce controls.

00:03:16.094 --> 00:03:17.010
Let's protect the API.

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:23.440
This rule helps in safeguarding
the inbound traffic

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:25.310
to the shipping service
with an API key.

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:28.400
As a next step, we'll
apply that rule.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.490
Developers can't access the
API without a key, which they

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:33.470
can get by creating an app.

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:35.220
Let's go back to the
developer portal.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:39.350
Log in as a developer.

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:41.270
Create an app and
access the API.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:49.840
This application will be
called as Hipster Demo App

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:52.828
and will copy the key
that is generated.

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:01.270
If you access the API as
a developer without a key,

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:04.510
it should fail, as we don't
have the authorization headers.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:06.760
Now, let's make the same
call with the right header

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:08.800
to be able to read through
the tracking details

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:09.924
to the successful response.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:16.589
By doing all of
this, you can see

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:18.899
that Apigee is doing the
API key verifications,

00:04:18.899 --> 00:04:21.584
security is enforced,
and the API key is valid.

00:04:21.584 --> 00:04:22.500
STEPHANIE WONG: Great.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:24.940
So with Apigee I
can manage my APIs

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:27.699
so other developers can build
applications on top of mine.

00:04:27.699 --> 00:04:28.490
MARUTI C.: Exactly.

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:30.750
Istio and Apigee can help
you build your application

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:32.610
and make it too cool for school.

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:34.990
If you like what you saw,
make sure to like, comment,

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:35.574
and subscribe.

00:04:35.574 --> 00:04:38.031
STEPHANIE WONG: And if you'd
like to get in contact with us

00:04:38.031 --> 00:04:40.350
or know more about Istio,
Apigee, and microservices

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:43.320
click on the links
in the description.

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:45.770
[MUSIC PLAYING]

